<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yoshino, Nara</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yoshino is a mountain town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> Prefecture, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, famous for the cherry trees of Mount Yoshino and for its shrines and temples on the pilgrimage routes of the Kii Mountains. In 1336 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-daigo">Emperor Go-Daigo</a> fled there with the imperial regalia and set up the Southern Court, which rivalled the Northern Court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> until 1392.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yoshino-nara.jpg" alt="Yoshino, Nara" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 水だらけのプール · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yoshino_Montage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Yoshino (吉野町, Yoshino-chō) is a town in Yoshino District, in the central part of Nara Prefecture. The Ryumon Mountains run east to west in the north, the Omine Mountain Range spreads out to the south, and mountains extending from the Daidaka range lie to the east. Between them the Yoshino River flows from southeast to west through the centre of the town, and the Takami River flows from east to west. Settlements lie on the narrow stretches of flat land inside the meanders of the Yoshino River and on the more open land along its tributaries. The climate is humid subtropical, with an average annual temperature of 13.0 °C and about 1,893 millimetres of rain a year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was part of ancient Yamato Province. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kojiki">Kojiki</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nihon-shoki">Nihon Shoki</a> mention Yoshino as a stopover on Emperor Jimmu's eastward journey to conquer Yamato. In the Asuka period, because it lay directly south of the capitals Asuka-kyō, Fujiwara-kyō and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heijo-kyo">Heijō-kyō</a>, it was a popular recreational spot for Emperor Ojin and other emperors and aristocrats, and Yoshino Palace stood there. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> period the area was sacred to adherents of Shugendo. It was the capital of the Southern Court in the Nanboku-chō period.</p><p>After the Meiji restoration the village of Yoshino was established on 1 April 1889 under the modern municipal system. It became a town on 24 April 1928 and on 3 May 1956 annexed the town of Kamiichi and the villages of Kokusui, Nakasho, Ryumon and Nakaryumon. A local story holds that Prince Oama, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-tenmu">Emperor Tenmu</a>, taught the people of the Kuzu district to make washi paper in the 7th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mount Yoshino is known for its many thousands of cherry trees, which have inspired poetry by Chiyo, Uejima Onitsura and other writers. They were planted in four groves at different altitudes so that they come into bloom at different times in the spring. An account written about 1714 describes about 1,000 trees at the base, 1,000 in the middle, 1,000 higher up and 1,000 in the precincts of the inner shrine at the top.</p><p>The town has 5,874 people and covers 95.65 square kilometres. Its economy is based on forestry, sake brewing and tourism, and most of its forest is red cedar and cypress that have been planted and harvested in cycles for 500 years. Kinpusen-ji, Kinpu Shrine, Yoshino Shrine and Yoshino-Kumano National Park lie within it. In 2012 it was designated one of the Most Beautiful Villages in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yoshino, Nara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He defeated Kusunoki Masashige at Minatogawa in 1336, installed a rival emperor and founded the Ashikaga shogunate, while Go-Daigo fled to <b>Yoshino</b> to found the Southern Court.</blockquote><a href="/state/kenmu-restoration">Kenmu Restoration</a></li><li><blockquote>Go-Daigo fled with the sacred regalia to <b>Yoshino</b>, splitting the court in two.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-court">Northern Court</a></li><li><blockquote>After his Kenmu Restoration collapsed, Go-Daigo escaped from Kyoto late in 1336 and established his court at <b>Yoshino</b> in the mountains south of Nara, insisting that the regalia he carried made him the only rightful emperor.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-court">Southern Court</a></li></ul></section>
